<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Johann Ludwig Burckhardt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1784">1784</a>–<a href="/years/1817">1817</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Jean Louis Burckhardt</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Johann Ludwig Burckhardt was a Swiss traveller and orientalist who explored the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a> in the guise of a Muslim scholar, Sheikh Ibrahim. In 1812 he noticed inscribed stones at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hama">Hama</a>, later recognised as Hittite hieroglyphs, and became the first European in modern times to see <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petra">Petra</a>. He also visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-simbel">Abu Simbel</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> before dying of dysentery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> in 1817.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/johann-ludwig-burckhardt.jpg" alt="Johann Ludwig Burckhardt" width="320" height="379" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Johann_Ludwig_Burckhardt.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Burckhardt was born on 24 November 1784 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lausanne">Lausanne</a>, into a wealthy Basel family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> merchants. After studying at the universities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottingen">Göttingen</a> he travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in the summer of 1806, hoping for a post in the civil service. Unsuccessful, he took employment with the African Association, which wanted the course of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger River</a> explained. The expedition called for an overland journey from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a>, and to prepare for it he attended Cambridge University, studied Arabic, science and medicine, and began to adopt Arabian costume.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1809 he left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> to perfect his Arabic and his knowledge of Muslim customs. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> he took the alias Sheikh Ibrahim Ibn Abdallah, investigated local languages and archaeological sites, and became the first European discoverer of Hittite or Luwian hieroglyphs. He was robbed more than once by men he had paid for protection. After more than two years he tested his disguise on three journeys through Syria, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a> and Transjordan, then left Aleppo in early 1812 and headed south.</p><p>On 22 August 1812, on the road towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>, he was led through a narrow valley and became the first modern European to see the Nabataean city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petra">Petra</a>. Fearing exposure as a treasure-seeking unbeliever, he could not stay long or take detailed notes. He reached Cairo on 4 September 1812. In March 1813, travelling in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a>, he found the sand-choked Great Temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-simbel">Abu Simbel</a> but could not clear its entrance. He told Giovanni Belzoni, who returned in 1817 and excavated it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Burckhardt entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeddah">Jeddah</a> on 18 July 1814 and was permitted to travel to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, where he spent several months performing the rituals of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajj">Hajj</a>, which was unheard of for a European. His detailed observations later served Richard Burton. He spent his last two years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> editing his journals and preparing for the caravan across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a>. He died there of dysentery on 15 October 1817, never having made his journey to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a>, and was buried as a Muslim under a tombstone bearing his assumed name.</p><p>He bequeathed 800 volumes of oriental <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> to the library of Cambridge University. The African Association published his works after his death, among them Travels in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> (1819), Travels in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-land">Holy Land</a> (1822) and Travels in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> (1829).</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Johann Ludwig Burckhardt” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its early rulers wrote in Luwian hieroglyphs; carved stones from the city, noticed by <b>Johann Ludwig Burckhardt</b> in 1812, were the first Hittite hieroglyphs known to modern scholars.</blockquote><a href="/state/hamath">Hamath</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lausanne">Lausanne</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/switzerland">Switzerland</a>. Died in Cairo, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=46.53,6.63&amp;year=1805" aria-label="Lausanne on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4706 528 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Johann Ludwig Burckhardt"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5156 798h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5156 798h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=46.53,6.63&amp;year=1805">What was here in 1805? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
